## 3.2 Data Preparation

The code chunk below is used to aggregate Sales and Budgeted Sales at the Product level:



::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
product <- coffeechain %>% 
  group_by(Product) %>%
  summarise(target = sum(`Budget Sales`),
            current = sum(Sales)) %>%
  ungroup()
```
:::



# 4 Plotting Bullet chart & sparklines using ggplot2

## 4.1 Plotting Bullet chart

The code chunk below is used to plot the bullet charts using ggplot2 functions.

The plot shows the sales target by product (red error bar) along with 75% and 85% of the target sales:



::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(product, aes(Product, current)) + 
  geom_col(aes(Product, max(target) * 1.01),
           fill="grey85", width=0.85) +
  geom_col(aes(Product, target * 0.75),
           fill="grey60", width=0.85) +
  geom_col(aes(Product, target * 0.5),
           fill="grey50", width=0.85) +
  geom_col(aes(Product, current), 
           width=0.35,
           fill = "black") + 
  geom_errorbar(aes(y = target,
                    x = Product, 
                    ymin = target,
                    ymax= target), 
                width = .4,
                colour = "red",
                size = 1) +
  coord_flip()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](Hands-on_Ex10_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-9-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::



## 4.2 Plotting Sparklines

### 4.2.1 Prepare the data

The code chunk below is to aggregate sales by Month and product:



::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
sales_report <- coffeechain %>%
  filter(Date >= "2013-01-01") %>%
  mutate(Month = month(Date)) %>%
  group_by(Month, Product) %>%
  summarise(Sales = sum(Sales)) %>%
  ungroup() %>%
  select(Month, Product, Sales)
```
:::



The code chunk below is used to compute the minimum, maximum and end othe the month sales:

-   `slice()`: selects rows from the grouped data.

-   `which.min(Sales)` finds the index of the minimum value in the Sales column for each product group.



::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
mins <- group_by(sales_report, Product) %>% 
  slice(which.min(Sales))
maxs <- group_by(sales_report, Product) %>% 
  slice(which.max(Sales))
ends <- group_by(sales_report, Product) %>% 
  filter(Month == max(Month))
```
:::



The code chunk below is used to compute the 25 and 75 quantiles:



::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
quarts <- sales_report %>%
  group_by(Product) %>%
  summarise(quart1 = quantile(Sales, 0.25),
            quart2 = quantile(Sales, 0.75)) %>%
  right_join(sales_report)
```
:::



### 4.2.2 sparklines in ggplot2

The sparkline plot displays monthly sales, highlighting maximum and minimum values, and shows sales in December by product.

The code below uses `geom_ribbon` to compare total sales amounts, where the higher the sales, the wider the ribbon:



::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(sales_report, aes(x=Month, y=Sales)) + 
  facet_grid(Product ~ ., scales = "free_y") + 
  geom_ribbon(data = quarts, aes(ymin = quart1, max = quart2), 
              fill = 'grey80') +
  geom_line(size=0.3) +
  geom_point(data = mins, col = 'red') +
  geom_point(data = maxs, col = 'blue') +
  geom_text(data = mins, aes(label = Sales), vjust = -1) +
  geom_text(data = maxs, aes(label = Sales), vjust = 2.5) +
  geom_text(data = ends, aes(label = Sales), hjust = 0, nudge_x = 0.35) +
  geom_text(data = ends, aes(label = Product), hjust = 0, nudge_x = 1.0) +
  expand_limits(x = max(sales_report$Month) + 
                  (0.25 * (max(sales_report$Month) - min(sales_report$Month)))) +
  scale_x_continuous(breaks = seq(1, 12, 1)) +
  scale_y_continuous(expand = c(0.1, 0)) +
  labs(title= "Monthly Sales Breakdown by Product")+
  theme_tufte(base_size = 3, base_family = "Helvetica") +
  theme(plot.title = element_text(size=15,hjust=0.5,face="bold"),
        axis.title=element_blank(), axis.text.y = element_blank(), 
        axis.ticks = element_blank(), strip.text = element_blank(),
        plot.background=element_rect(fill="#f3f1e9",colour="#f3f1e9"))
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](Hands-on_Ex10_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-13-1.png){width=1152}
:::
:::



# 5 Static Information Dashboard Design: gt and gtExtras methods

In this section, we will learn how to create static information dashboard by using [gt](https://gt.rstudio.com/) and [gtExtras](https://jthomasmock.github.io/gtExtras/index.html) packages.

## 5.1 Plotting a simple bullet chart



::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
product %>%
  gt::gt() %>%
  gt_plt_bullet(column = current, 
              target = target, 
              width = 60,
              palette = c("#A4A9D9", 
                          "black")) %>%
gt_theme_espn()
```

